_summary: Wake on Wireless LAN (WoWLAN) test from S3 - {interface} - wakeonlan
_purpose:
 Check that another system can wake up the Device Under Test (DUT) from S3 using the WoWLAN function.
_steps:
 1. Ensure WoWLAN is enabled in BIOS.
 2. Initiate a connection to an Access Point (using nmcli).
 3. Configure the device for WoWLAN, run the command:
    $ sudo iw phy phy0 wowlan enable magic-packet
 4. Press Enter to suspend the system.
 5. From another computer, send a magic packet to wake the DUT:
    $ wakeonlan {mac}
    This defaults to 255.255.255.255 and only reaches the local subnet.
    If sending from a different subnet or VLAN, specify the DUT's IP
    address or its subnet's directed broadcast address with -i:
    $ wakeonlan -i <TARGET_IP_OR_BROADCAST> {mac}
    For reliable unicast delivery to the DUT's IP, ensure the last-hop
    router has a static or persistent ARP/neighbor mapping for the DUT.
    Dynamic entries may expire while the DUT is asleep and block delivery.
    If this cannot be ensured, use the directed broadcast address instead.
    For example, for a target subnet of 192.168.10.0/24:
    $ wakeonlan -i 192.168.10.255 {mac}
    Ensure routing and firewalls allow the magic packet to reach the DUT.
    If using a directed broadcast address, routers must allow forwarding
    directed broadcasts to the target subnet.
    If the wakeonlan tool is not installed, run:
    $ sudo apt install wakeonlan
_verification:
 Did the DUT wake up from S3?
plugin: user-interact-verify
requires:
  sleep.mem == 'supported'
command: systemctl suspend
user: root
category_id: com.canonical.plainbox::wireless
estimated_duration: 120
flags: preserve-locale

